in @prism
in @meta
in @glass
in @epistemologic
in @epistemologic/cybernetic

# @epistemologic/cybernetic/variety — the substrate's first cybernetic
# property. Ashby 1956 §11/7, lifted to the multi-dimensional reading
# (recognition #36, mirror `1ad45b4`).
#
# === The Ashby claim, verbatim ===
#
# Ashby, W. R. (1956). *An Introduction to Cybernetics*. Chapman & Hall.
# §11/7 (the Law of Requisite Variety):
#
#   Variety is the count of distinguishable states. A regulator R with
#   variety V(R) can fully regulate a disturbed system D with variety
#   V(D) only if V(R) ≥ V(D). *Only variety can destroy variety.*
#
# The complementary substrate-pull recognition (Reed, 2026-06-09; mirror
# `1ad45b4`): variety is a *vector*, not a scalar. Two languages can be
# ordered on one component and reversed on another. The folk reading
# "Turing-complete = strictly more expressive than sub-Turing" projects
# multi-dimensional variety onto axis 1 (computational) and LOSES the
# other components. Mirror's deliberate trade is sub-Turing on axis 1 to
# buy variety on axes 2–5 (type, effect, proof, epistemologic) that
# Turing-complete languages have no remaining variety budget for.
#
# This property declares the substrate's instantiation of that claim:
# the per-shard variety budget, vector-typed, verifiable at the species
# boundary.
#
# === The 4-way bilateral mapping ===
#
# Per the cybernetic foundation §4.1, the property/operation seam
# (`@epistemologic/cybernetic/variety` here / `@mirror/cybernetics/variety`
# pending) maps to four cybernetic traditions simultaneously. For
# *variety* specifically: this property IS von Foerster's second-order
# observation (the shard's claim ABOUT ITS OWN variety budget); IS
# Maturana's organisation (the variety vector is the pattern that makes
# the substrate THAT substrate); IS Beer's S4 intelligence (the substrate-
# wide reasoning about what budget each shard holds across time); IS
# Pask's P-individual identity (each shard's variety vector is its
# substrate-altitude conversational vocabulary). Four distinctions
# converging at one architectural seam is what makes the substrate's
# cybernetic instantiation novel; the literature names each distinction
# in isolation, the substrate runs all four at once.
#
# === The verifiable shape (per cybernetic foundation §3.5 row 1) ===
#
# - Declares the shard's variety vector with per-axis budget.
# - Verifiable at species boundary via `@code/metalogue/materialize.classify`.
# - The per-axis loss profile is recoverable from the opacity-weighted
#   projection at the boundary (`shards/glass.mirror`'s `opacity_map`
#   carries the residuals; per-axis labelling is the interpretive
#   overlay that lands at `@epistemologic/cybernetic/axis` if a consumer
#   pulls).
# - What stays prose-only at this tick: the axis labels as a typed
#   carrier. Declared abstractly here so shards can name their vectors;
#   the labels' enforcement at compile-time lands when verification
#   pulls.
#
# === No operational sibling THIS tick ===
#
# Per [[feedback-craft-not-deliver]] the operational sibling at
# `@mirror/cybernetics/variety` is FORWARD-PROMISED, not landed. The
# property at this altitude declares the obligation; the operation (the
# substrate-altitude variety computation, if any consumer needs one)
# lands at species realisation when a consumer pulls. Distinct from
# `@epistemologic/cybernetic/design` (Glanville), which per the
# foundation §4.3 has NO operational sibling at all by construction;
# `variety` has a sibling that just hasn't been pulled yet.
#
# === Pre-AI prior art ===
#
#   variety / requisite variety  Ashby 1956 §11/7. The original. The
#                                complementary book is Ashby (1952),
#                                *Design for a Brain*, which develops
#                                the homeostat — the four-unit
#                                electromechanical proof of requisite
#                                variety via ultrastable random
#                                reorganisation.
#   variety as vector            The substrate's multi-dimensional
#                                lift (recognition #36, 2026-06-09).
#                                Pre-AI lineage at the literature
#                                level: Conant 1976 (IEEE T-SMC 6(4))
#                                decomposes a multi-component system's
#                                information rate into orthogonal
#                                components — the same orthogonal-axes
#                                discipline this property carries at
#                                the language-design altitude.
#   axis decomposition           Shannon 1948 (Bell Syst Tech J 27);
#                                Conant 1976 (the multi-component
#                                extension cited above). The substrate's
#                                contribution is *naming the language-
#                                level axes* (computational, type,
#                                effect, proof, epistemologic) as the
#                                load-bearing decomposition.
#
# The substrate's contribution is *typing* the variety vector so every
# shard's per-axis budget is substrate-fact rather than prose, and the
# species-boundary projection's loss profile is mechanically tracked
# via the existing `transparency<p>` and `opacity_map` machinery.
#
# Spec context: docs/insights/2026-06-09-ashby-multi-dimensional-variety-
#               sub-turing-epistemologic.md (the recognition);
#               systemic.engineering `f9e0402`
#               (practice/insights/cybernetics/
#                2026-06-09-cybernetic-foundation-for-mirror-substrate.md),
#               §2.1 (Variety as vector — Ashby 1956 §11/7), §3.5 row 1
#               (the verifiable shape), §5.3 item 1 (substrate-pull
#               urgency rationale for landing this first).
# Substrate decisions: [[architecture-shards-as-substrate-source]],
#                      [[architecture-prism-as-trait-as-everything]],
#                      [[architecture-glass-wall-substrate-types]],
#                      [[architecture-kintsugi-variety-io]] (prior
#                       Ashby citation; this property is the multi-axis
#                       lift of the same discipline),
#                      [[feedback-no-bare-types]] (axis_budget is a
#                       typed carrier, not a bare scalar),
#                      [[feedback-substrate-already-had-the-word]]
#                       (variety as the carrier of structure already
#                       implicit in `variety_hold` on @fate; this
#                       property names the discipline at the substrate
#                       altitude).
# Related shards: epistemologic/cybernetic.mirror (the family root),
#                 glass.mirror (the @glass altitude this property's
#                 verdicts surface against; `transparency<p>` and
#                 `opacity_map` are the recovery machinery for the
#                 per-axis loss profile),
#                 code/metalogue/materialize.mirror (the species-
#                 boundary recognitive discriminator the verification
#                 routes through; retired from @mirror/realisation
#                 2026-06-10).
# Path-namespace property: this file at
# `shards/epistemologic/cybernetic/variety.mirror` declares
# `@epistemologic/cybernetic/variety` per
# `@epistemologic/property/path_matches_namespace`.

prism @epistemologic/cybernetic/variety {
  focus variety
  project variety
  split variety
  shift variety
  settle variety
}

# === The five canonical axes ===
#
# Per recognition #36, variety decomposes along at minimum five axes for
# programming-language substrates. Each axis is independent; the language-
# level variety budget is the vector across all five.
#
# Species shards MAY extend with additional axes when their carrier
# discipline names a sixth dimension (e.g. an audio-altitude shard might
# add `acoustic`, a network-altitude shard might add `topological`). The
# extension mechanism is per-species declaration of additional `axis`
# variants at the species altitude; the substrate's five are the floor.
#
# - computational  what functions can be expressed. Turing-completeness
#                  is maximal here. Mirror surrenders this axis to buy
#                  the others.
# - type           what invariants the type system can express. The
#                  C → Rust → Haskell → Idris monotone-increasing climb.
# - effect         what side-effects the type system can track. Koka
#                  effects, algebraic effects, monad transformers.
# - proof          what propositions the system can decide or verify.
#                  Coq, Agda, Lean — dependent types as the variety
#                  carrier.
# - epistemologic  what KINDS of knowledge the system can represent.
#                  Gaps, verdicts, transparencies, obligations,
#                  conversational structure, settlement, kintsugi loops.
#                  Mirror's purchase axis.
type axis =
  | computational
  | type_axis
  | effect
  | proof
  | epistemologic

# === The per-axis budget carrier ===
#
# A typed carrier per [[feedback-no-bare-types]]: the budget is its own
# newtype with the axis it applies to recorded alongside the value. A
# consumer collecting the per-axis budget map can rank without losing
# axis identity.
#
# `value` carries the budget scalar as a `ref` (matches
# `sheaf_laplacian.eigenvalue.value` and `curvature.value` precedents).
# The interpretation of `value` is per-axis: bits-of-distinguishable-
# states for the computational axis; type-invariant rank for the type
# axis; effect-row cardinality for the effect axis; proposition-class
# rank for the proof axis; substrate-primitive count for the
# epistemologic axis. Each species' realisation closes the
# interpretation; the substrate names the carrier.
#
# Identity contract: byte-equality on (axis, value). The realisation
# layer enforces this; the substrate names it.
type axis_budget = {
  axis:  axis,
  value: ref,
}

# === The variety vector ===
#
# A shard's variety vector IS its per-axis budget across the five
# canonical axes (plus any species extensions). The carrier is a list of
# `axis_budget` records — matching the @glass `opacity_map = [opacity]`
# precedent for accumulated per-site data.
#
# Identity contract: the vector's identity IS the multiset of its
# components. Two vectors with byte-equal multisets ARE the same vector
# under the substrate's content-addressing.
type variety_vector = [axis_budget]

# === The per-axis loss profile at the species boundary ===
#
# When a shard crosses a species boundary (per
# `@code/metalogue/materialize.classify`), the projection at the boundary
# may lose variety on some axis. The loss profile is recoverable from
# the opacity-weighted projection —
# `shards/glass.mirror`'s `opacity_map` carries the residuals; the
# per-axis loss is the slice of that map labelled by axis.
#
# `axis_loss(s, species, axis)` reads the residual variety lost on
# `axis` when shard `s` is projected to `species`. The verdict is
# `transparency<axis_budget>` — the substrate's loss carrier parameterized
# on the axis being read. `success` means the projection preserves the
# declared budget on this axis; `partial(opacity_map)` names where the
# preservation was lossy; `failure(opacity_map)` names where the
# preservation broke entirely.
#
# Body discharges per-species at the realisation boundary. Same shape as
# the four metalogue shims at `shards/code/metalogue.mirror`: the
# substrate declares the obligation; species bodies discharge when a
# consumer pulls.
axis_loss(s: shard, species: ref, axis: axis) -> transparency(axis) { \ }

# === The shard's declared variety vector ===
#
# `declared_variety(s)` reads the variety vector a shard declares for
# itself — the substrate's second-order observation made first-class.
# A shard that doesn't name its vector is treated as carrying the
# substrate floor (the five canonical axes at unit budget); a shard
# that names its vector explicitly is making the Ashby trade declared.
#
# Body discharges at the realisation boundary; the per-shard vector is
# substrate-fact once any consumer has read it through this action.
#
# Named `declared_variety` rather than `variety_vector` to avoid the
# type/action name collision and to make the second-order semantics
# legible: the action READS what the shard CLAIMS; the type IS what
# the shard HAS.
declared_variety(s: shard) -> variety_vector { \ }

# === variety_preserving — the bilateral preservation predicate ===
#
# `variety_preserving(species)` is the predicate consumer shards name in
# a `requires` clause to declare their bilateral agreement — per
# recognition #37 (the Pask-agreement reading of `requires`) — that the
# projection at the species boundary admits a known loss profile on each
# declared axis. The agreement is bilateral because both sides commit:
# the shard commits to declaring its vector via `declared_variety`; the
# species commits to bounding the per-axis loss within the declared
# budget under `axis_loss`. The verdict at the boundary IS the audit of
# the bilateral commitment.
#
# Declared here as a typed predicate so downstream shards can write
# `requires variety_preserving(@code/rust)` or `requires
# variety_preserving(@code/mirror)` — same shape as the metalogue
# `requires substrate_pull_preserving(shim_X)` pattern at
# `shards/code/metalogue.mirror`. The body is `\` at this altitude; the
# discharge IS the conjunction of per-axis `axis_loss` verdicts falling
# inside the declared budget at the species boundary.
#
# No `requires variety_preserving(…)` clauses are added at THIS shard:
# the property declares the predicate; consumers add the clauses when
# they pull. Per [[feedback-craft-not-deliver]] the operational sibling
# at `@mirror/cybernetics/variety` lands the same way.
variety_preserving(species: ref) -> verdict { \ }

out @epistemologic/cybernetic/variety
out axis
out axis_budget
out variety_vector
out axis_loss
out declared_variety
out variety_preserving
